          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: सूनुः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "सूनुः (sūnuḥ) — \"son\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "From √सू (sū), 'to bring forth, to give birth' — so the word means the one who was brought forth. Its cousins are Gothic sunus, Old English sunu, English son, Lithuanian sunus, Russian syn. Sanskrit's other son-word, putraḥ, is far commoner in the texts and has no such relatives; this quieter one is the inherited word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three of the four, and a root that names a person by what happened to them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
